import java.util.Scanner;

public class StepCounter {
    public static double stepsToMiles(int steps) throws Exception {
        if (steps < 0) {
            throw new Exception("Exception: Negative step count entered.");
        }

        return steps / 2000.0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scnr = new Scanner(System.in);
        int steps = scnr.nextInt();

        try {
            double miles = stepsToMiles(steps);
            System.out.printf("%.2f%n", miles);
        }
        catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }

        scnr.close();
    }
}

## Check Your Understanding 

1) A double preserves fractional miles and decimal values.
2) A negative step count is invalid because steps walked cannot be negative.
3) throws Exception indicates that the method may throw an exception that must be handled or declared by the calling code.
4) An exception thrown inside the try block transfers control to the matching catch block.
5) Use e.getMessage() to retrieve the exception message.
6) Integer division discards the fractional part, while floating-point division preserves it.
